## Phase 2 — Transport adapter interface

**Goal:** define the 4-method contract that all transports must implement.

```js
// transport.js — every adapter exports this shape
push(job)       // job = { id, gcode, meta }  → Promise
poll()          // → Promise<{ status, position, error }>
beacon(event)   // event = { jobId, line, type } → Promise
history(n)      // → Promise<Job[]>
```

### Transport tiers (in order of user-friendliness)

**Tier 1 — fairy-direct (no cloud, no accounts)**

|Adapter                  |When to use                                          |
|-------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|
|`transports/localhost.js`|Same PC as fairy (dev, or Studio on shop PC)         |
|`transports/lan.js`      |Same WiFi — `https://cnc-fairy.local`                |
|`transports/direct.js`   |Anywhere — user’s own domain/DuckDNS via port forward|

All three use the **same fairy HTTPS API** — only the base URL differs. `direct.js` is essentially `lan.js` with a configurable hostname.

**Tier 2 — user cloud (for networks the user can’t configure)**

|Adapter                   |Auth             |Notes                           |
|--------------------------|-----------------|--------------------------------|
|`transports/cloudflare.js`|API token        |Frédéric’s stack, reference impl|
|`transports/dropbox.js`   |OAuth2           |Easiest for casual users        |
|`transports/gdrive.js`    |OAuth2           |Google ecosystem users          |
|`transports/onedrive.js`  |OAuth2           |Microsoft ecosystem users       |
|`transports/supabase.js`  |Connection string|Best realtime option            |
|`transports/s3.js`        |API key          |Covers AWS, Backblaze B2, Wasabi|

